  "title": "CHAPTER 2- CELL",
  "order": 2,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "22kvm9w",
      "content": "<strong>1. Introduction to Cell</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "statStrip",
      "stats": [
        {
          "label": "First Discovered",
          "value": "1665 (Robert Hooke)",
          "icon": "calendar"
        },
        {
          "label": "Cell Theory",
          "value": "1839 (Schleiden & Schwann)",
          "icon": "book"
        },
        {
          "label": "Smallest Cell",
          "value": "Mycoplasma (0.3 µm)",
          "icon": "info"
        },
        {
          "label": "Powerhouse",
          "value": "Mitochondria (ATP)",
          "icon": "check"
        }
      ],
      "id": "1nvbl2r"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "The cell is the fundamental structural and functional unit of all living organisms.",
        "Robert Hooke discovered cells in 1665, while Anton von Leeuwenhoek first observed live cells.",
        "Schleiden, Schwann, and Virchow formulated the Cell Theory stating all organisms consist of cells arising from pre-existing cells.",
        "Prokaryotic cells lack membrane-bound nuclei and organelles, whereas eukaryotic cells possess a compartmentalized structure.",
        "Mitochondria generate cellular energy in the form of ATP, while lysosomes contain hydrolytic enzymes for cellular digestion."
      ],
      "id": "mmqkits"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "psz28in",
      "content": "The cell is the basic structural and functional unit of life. All living organisms are composed of one or more cells. Every life process such as respiration, digestion, excretion, growth and reproduction occurs at the cellular level."
    },
    {
      "id": "en7z764",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "ekyumms",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_ekyumms.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "id": "bte9nhs",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "y1b8hbc",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_y1b8hbc.jpeg"
    },
    {
      "id": "nh2w1ak",
      "type": "image",
      "imageId": "liaj2mq",
      "src": "/books/Precis/RRB GS/images/image_liaj2mq.png"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tqir3dn",
      "content": "1.Cell was discovered by Robert Hooke in 1665 while observing cork."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "pmy8guw",
      "content": "2.The nucleus was discovered by Robert Brown in 1831."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3j95wph",
      "content": "3. The smallest living cell is Mycoplasma (0.1–0.5 µm)."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "4ki8kq7",
      "content": "4.The largest cell in the world is the Ostrich egg."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "xkz05o9",
      "content": "5.Human body contains trillions of cells.<strong><br></br>2. Cell Theory</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "zfull35",
      "content": "Cell theory was proposed by Matthias Schleiden and Theodor Schwann and later modified by Rudolf Virchow."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3nofooc",
      "content": "1.All living organisms are made up of cells."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gvh02na",
      "content": "2.Cell is the basic structural and functional unit of life."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "apgjnw7",
      "content": "3.New cells arise from pre-existing cells."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "28dzjox",
      "content": "<strong><br></br>3. Types of Cells</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "gyuujx3",
      "content": "<strong>A. Prokaryotic Cells</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ljnnsbc",
      "content": "Prokaryotic cells lack a true nucleus and membrane-bound organelles. The genetic material is present in a nucleoid region."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hgvjkhw",
      "content": "•Example: Bacteria, Cyanobacteria."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "0g5lztz",
      "content": "•Size: 1–10 micrometer."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "524xwok",
      "content": "•DNA is circular."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "d7ioks8",
      "content": "<strong>B. Eukaryotic Cells</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "s9d5gd8",
      "content": "Eukaryotic cells contain a well-defined nucleus surrounded by a nuclear membrane. They possess membrane-bound organelles."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "km1shbh",
      "content": "• Example: Plant cells, Animal cells, Fungi."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "3e8hbrs",
      "content": "• Size: 10–100 micrometer."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gwotxfg",
      "content": "• DNA is linear and organized into chromosomes."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "a3wsdbg",
      "content": "<strong>Comparison Between Prokaryotic and Eukaryotic Cells</strong>"
    },
    {
      "id": "c3230p6",
      "type": "table",
