            "<p>Present</p>",
            "<p>Absent</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Chloroplast</p>",
            "<p>Present</p>",
            "<p>Absent</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Vacuole</p>",
            "<p>Large central vacuole</p>",
            "<p>Small or absent</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Shape</p>",
            "<p>Rectangular</p>",
            "<p>Round/Irregular</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "v3axten",
      "content": "<strong><br></br>7. Cell Division</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ddqzi37",
      "content": "<strong>Mitosis:</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "jk9n97a",
      "content": "Mitosis is an equational division producing two identical daughter cells."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "e1qx0c5",
      "content": "•Occurs in somatic cells."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qe9gagy",
      "content": "•Responsible for growth and repair."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "iboly5g",
      "content": "<strong>Meiosis:</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "thbpr1d",
      "content": "Meiosis is a reduction division producing four daughter cells with half chromosome number."
    },
    {
      "type": "pullQuote",
      "content": "Cell is the fundamental structural and functional unit of all living organisms.",
      "id": "rgqo2lr"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "g85owm2",
      "content": "1.Occurs in reproductive cells."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "kkq8s67",
      "content": "2.Responsible for formation of gametes."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wadeh55",
      "content": "3. Introduces genetic variation."
    },
    {
      "id": "lnon9tp",
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        {
          "isHeader": true,
          "cells": [
            "<p><strong>Feature</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Mitosis</strong></p>",
            "<p><strong>Meiosis</strong></p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Number of Divisions</p>",
            "<p>One</p>",
            "<p>Two</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Daughter Cells</p>",
            "<p>Two</p>",
            "<p>Four</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Chromosome Number</p>",
            "<p>Same</p>",
            "<p>Half</p>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "isHeader": false,
          "cells": [
            "<p>Function</p>",
            "<p>Growth &amp; Repair</p>",
            "<p>Gamete Formation</p>"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "7a7zygl",
      "content": "<strong>8. Cytoskeleton</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "hzyux5m",
      "content": "Cytoskeleton is a network of protein filaments present in cytoplasm."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "meyi4ae",
      "content": "1.Provides structural support to the cell."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "msbs5be",
      "content": "2. Helps in movement of organelles."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tq3c65h",
      "content": "3. Maintains cell shape."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "qdpad5s",
      "content": "4. Composed of microtubules, microfilaments and intermediate filaments."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "a4sugs4",
      "content": "<strong><br></br>9. Centrosome and Centrioles</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "j7cs2hv",
      "content": "1. Present mainly in animal cells."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "crsvmjv",
      "content": "2. Help in spindle formation during cell division."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "5c9l8g9",
      "content": "3. Absent in most plant cells."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "g1pa1y0",
      "content": "<strong><br></br>10. Cilia and Flagella</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "71o32p3",
      "content": "1.Hair-like projections from cell surface."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "21kasi3",
      "content": "2. Help in locomotion."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "m0x1csr",
      "content": "3.Structure based on 9+2 arrangement of microtubules."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "tdsdxn2",
      "content": "<strong><br></br>11. Fluid Mosaic Model</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "k2sp3wk",
      "content": "1. Proposed by Singer and Nicolson (1972)."
    },
    {
      "type": "comparisonTable",
      "headers": [
        "Feature",
        "Prokaryotic Cell",
        "Eukaryotic Cell"
      ],
      "rows": [
        [
          "Nuclear Structure",
          "Nucleoid (no nuclear membrane)",
          "True nucleus with nuclear envelope"
        ],
        [
          "Membrane-bound Organelles",
          "Absent",
          "Present (ER, Golgi, Mitochondria)"
        ],
        [
          "Ribosome Size",
          "70S",
          "80S (Cytoplasm) / 70S (Organelles)"
        ],
        [
          "Cell Division",
          "Binary Fission",
          "Mitosis & Meiosis"
        ]
      ],
      "id": "hq36f32"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "d3j61yz",
      "content": "2. Explains structure of plasma membrane."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "il1y8sd",
      "content": "3.Membrane consists of phospholipid bilayer with embedded proteins."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "8lc95e6",
      "content": "<strong><br></br>12. Cell Inclusion Bodies</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "raz5c9f",
      "content": "1.Non-living substances present in cytoplasm."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "u02nt2z",
      "content": " Examples: Glycogen granules, fat droplets, pigments."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ka4ora2",
      "content": "<strong><br></br>13. Surface Area to Volume Ratio</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "f4x8rlh",
      "content": "1. Smaller cells have higher surface area to volume ratio."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tzmpkah",
      "content": "2. Helps in efficient exchange of materials."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "n8in6m3",
      "content": "3.As cell size increases, efficiency decreases."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "c5c7wg0",
      "content": "<strong><br></br>14. Cell Cycle Phases</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "wrfbf60",
      "content": "1.Interphase: G1, S, G2 phases.                                                                                                                                                    "
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "fryprr9",
      "content": "2. M phase: Mitotic phase."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "mmylp1r",
      "content": "3. DNA replication occurs in S phase."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "lswarkc",
      "content": "4. Maximum time spent in interphase."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "edr7w40",
      "content": "<strong><br></br>15. Stages of Mitosis</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ujrplcp",
      "content": "1.Prophase: Chromosomes condense."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "cueavl8",
      "content": "2. Metaphase: Chromosomes align at equator."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "rozwlei",
      "content": "3.Anaphase: Sister chromatids separate."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "dx9uiu0",
      "content": "4.Telophase: Nuclear membrane reappears."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "40bux08",
      "content": "5.Cytokinesis: Division of cytoplasm."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "0hrp9rb",
      "content": "<strong><br></br>16. Stages of Meiosis</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "y109m4t",
      "content": "1.Meiosis I: Reduction division."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "p7tbtux",
      "content": "2.Meiosis II: Similar to mitosis."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "joonnu6",
      "content": "3. Crossing over occurs in Prophase I."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "ydjmwyc",
      "content": "4. Leads to genetic variation."
    },
    {
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 4,
      "id": "bjkphrw",
      "content": "<strong><br></br>17. Apoptosis</strong>"
    },
    {
      "type": "examAlert",
      "items": [
        "Rudolf Virchow contributed the phrase 'Omnis cellula-e cellula' indicating all cells arise from pre-existing cells.",
        "Mitochondria and Chloroplasts are semi-autonomous organelles possessing their own circular DNA and 70S ribosomes.",
        "Lysosomes are frequently asked in exams as 'suicidal bags' due to their acid hydrolases.",
        "Ribosomes are unique non-membrane-bound organelles present in both prokaryotic and eukaryotic organisms."
      ],
      "id": "k30jhpi"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tqonqpt",
      "content": "1. Programmed cell death."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "tmclza1",
      "content": "2. Important for development and removal of damaged cells."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "gwcgxki",
      "content": "3. Controlled process, not harmful to surrounding cells."
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "id": "awnn4yu",
      "content": "<strong><br></br>18. Differences Between Apoptosis and Necrosis</strong>"
    },
